editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-133-She nodded, once, as to a thing entered. Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went out under the lintel at a carter's pace, on the low side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-135----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:137:Oryn walked out of that gate with her hands in the pockets of a dead man's coat, and she kept them there for the first mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:She did not often do that. A healer's hands are her eyes, and she carried hers open, as a rule, swinging, where the air could get at them. This evening they felt as they had felt once in her first year on a house's staff, when she had been set to wash a hundred bandages in a trough in the cold and had lost her fingers to the wrist; she could move them, and they would not tell her anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-141-She had put them on that boy three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-143-She had spent three mendings at the table since noon, the whole of a Tide's reserve for a day, and every one had been what it should be. The scald had stood in the arm like water behind a board. The ox-man's foot had been four small bones and a great deal of noise. She had gone into each of them as she went into a house she knew, and found what was stopped, and opened it, and come out tired. That was her trade. She had not once, since the morning her Path woke, laid a hand on a living body and had it come back to her empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-145-Three times, now. Once on a mountain, over three broken ribs, and twice on a scrubbed table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:147:She was not a person who guessed. She had been taught by a woman who struck her knuckles for guessing, and she had come to be glad of it. So she went over what her hands had found, as she would have gone over a patient for a second healer, and she kept strictly to that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-149-There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-151-*No banks.* She had told the small pale one so, since the small pale one was writing it down. It was the truest thing she had, and it was not much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:153:She tried her hands on the mule, at the first turn of the hill, as a person tries a lamp that has gone out once. She laid her palm on the warm brown neck, under the mane, and counted. It was all there. A beast's bed is broad and shallow and runs slow, and she had felt this one's every day for thirty rounds; it told her of a sound heart, and a near hind hoof that would want the smith inside the month. Her hands were as they had always been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-155-That ought to have been a comfort, and it was only half of one. If the fault was not in the instrument, then it lay in the thing the instrument had been put to, and she had no place to keep a thought of that kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-157-The road went up the hill in a long slant, and she went up it below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-159-Four people were coming down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-161-She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-173-He nodded. He had asked, and been told the price, and she saw him put it by. She gave him a nod of her own, as one gives it to a man who has not wasted one's time, and went on up the hill below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-175----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-177-She was at the next gate on the eighty-sixth, five days after she had said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-179-It was the hold that had given her the coat. She did there what she always did. She ate, and slept her two hours, and walked the bench; and at the end of it sat a man whose shoulder had come out of its place a week before, hauling a calf. His brothers had put it back for him, kneeling on his chest, since there was nobody else. It was in. It was not right. She spent two on it that should have been one, and she did not need to be told whose five days those were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:181:There was a woman on that bench, too, with a breast-fed child that would not thrive, and an old herdsman who wished to be told that the pain in his side was nothing. She told the one what to do and the other the truth. Neither of them cost her a mending, and both of them had needed her on the day; and that was the part of the round that nobody at a healing house would have understood, for a house counted what its hands did, and never what its eyes had spared them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-183-She gave that hold a night and a morning, and went on. They had put a loaf and a cold fowl in the off pannier without being asked, as they did every round, and the smith's girl had looked at the near hind hoof. It was how the coat had come to her. Nobody at any of the seven gates had ever spoken of a price, and nobody had ever let her leave with less than she came with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:The route was the only thing she had ever made. She thought of that on the road, where there was time. She had not been given it. There had been no round when she crossed the Line, only a rumour in a ferry-town that the holds had no hands; and she had gone to the first gate, and mended what was there, and asked where the next fence was. It had been four holds, the first year. She had put the fifth on in her third round, and the sixth for an old woman's sake. The seventh she had added because of a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-187-So she knew how a stop was put on. It was not done lightly. Every stop was two days, and every two days was a day that six other gates waited; she had sat on a chock-stone with a pencil in her teeth, the night she added the seventh, and worked it through until the round came out at twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-189-The child was at the next gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-191-She got there on the eighty-ninth evening, and she did not eat first. That was a rule broken, and she knew it, and she went straight through the yard to a house under a pear tree and put two fingers on a small wrist and two on a neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-193-Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-195-The banks were where they had always been, narrow and clear, a little ragged on the left side where the breath came hard in the cold. It was running. It was no worse than it had been twenty-five days ago, and perhaps a shade better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:197:She sat down on the floor of that house, and the child's mother put a bowl in her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:It had been nothing. She had been five days late, and for five days she had not known, and it had been nothing, this round. She ate the whole bowl. Her hands had told her the truth as easily as they ever had; they had gone in and come out full, and she sat and looked at them in the firelight with a relief that she would not have owned to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:201:It was not her hands, then. She had half thought, on the hill out of Oxhollow, that something in them might have begun to fail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-203-It was he.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-204-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-205-She left that hold at first light on the ninety-first, with two days of road in front of her and the mule going well. And somewhere on the first of those days, between a ford and a stand of the red trees, she found that she had been doing a sum for the better part of a morning without having set herself to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-206-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody. By what she had felt under her hand they would be sound in him some days sooner. But she could not read him, and so she could not know it, and she had never in her life lifted a tape on a guess. It would be the ninety-ninth, then, by the rule. The long one wanted him whole and still, and it wanted the better part of a day out of her besides, so that the hundredth was the first morning on which she could run it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-209-She would be at Thornwater on the ninety-third, and he would be fifteen days from the break. She would have her two days at that gate, and the carter's hand, which she owed, and whatever was on the bench; and on the ninety-fifth the round would take her out by the far road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:211:The hand troubled her more than she liked. A split across the palm is a small thing on the day, and she had left it clean and wrapped, and told the man to keep it out of a rein. He would not have kept it out of a rein. He had a load for Thornwater and one pair of hands, and he had given her the news of a late crew when he might have held his tongue and had his mending. By the ninety-third that hand would be fifteen days old. She owed it to him as she owed nothing else on the round, and she meant to pay it before she sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-213-So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-214-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-215-She walked a mile on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-216-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:217:She was not a person who wanted things. She had wanted one thing, once, which was to keep a list in the right order, and she had got it, and it had filled her days so full that there was no room left over. She had not thought that there was anything else her hands were hungry for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:219:They were hungry for this. She had spent her life reading what people were built like, and she had been proud of never meeting a body she could not go into. Now she had met one. She did not want to mend him; there was nothing to mend that a strip of linen could not see to. She wanted to lay both hands on him, whole and still, for as long as it took, and feel exactly how it was that he could not be read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-221-What he was she left alone. That was a question for people who kept books.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-223-On the morning of the ninety-second, on a level stretch with frost in the ruts, she did the thing she had done three times before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-225-She took it out of the round. It was five days from the ninety-fifth to the hundredth, and she went through them one at a time, as she went along a bench. Four she could spend at Thornwater's own table without lying to anybody. The grain was moving; carters came through that yard from every hold up the valley, and a bench that filled twice in two days would fill again in four. They would make her late at six gates all the same, and she knew every one of the people she would be keeping waiting. But they were work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-227-The fifth was not. On the hundredth she would mend nobody. She would spend a mending's worth of herself on a boy with nothing wrong with him, to learn a thing that would help no one on her route; and no hold owed her that day, and she owed it to none of them. If it was to be had, it must be added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-229-She put a day on the route.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-231-Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went on at the same pace as before, on the low side, toward a gate that was still a day's walk off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-233----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:235:It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three. He had done it before the healer's mule was out of hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-237-They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-239-Three. Whole, and fed at noon, and one of them no better off than a week ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-241-They had done two holds' work in four weeks, a drain cut and a byre roofed, and there was nothing in either that would be talked of at a plank. He did not mind that. Most of what kept a crew at the top of a sheet was work of that kind, done to the day and brought home without a mark on anybody; a man who wanted his lines to be stories did not keep his people long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-243-It was the bow's hip that he minded, and he put it first, since it was the cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-245-He had timed the second of his two slips to end at the upper hold on the eighty-first day. That was the day the healer's round should have brought her to its gate. He had said nothing of it to the bow; he had only found a day's more work on a byre roof that might have waited, and stayed. She had not come, and nobody at that gate could say why. On the hill she had given him no more than that she was late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-247-He did not blame her. A healer on a round is not late for nothing, and he had never known this one's reasons to be poor ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-248-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-250-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-251-The road came round the shoulder of the hill, and Oxhollow lay under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-252-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:253:He read the yard through the open gate as he went by it, without turning his head more than a walking man does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-255-There was no cart. That was the first thing, and it was the largest. He had heard on the road that they had hired the smith's, and there were three boxes standing against the gate-house wall on the bare ground, and a crew that has wheels does not stand its goods on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-257-The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-261-The small pale one was asleep sitting up, with her head on a box and a book shut under her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:263:There were four of them, and he had counted them rightly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-265-The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-267-"It's a no," he said. "Over the top. I've paid seventy to be told so, and I'd pay it again. They put their own cart in my gorge finding out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-269-"All five came down?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-271-"All five. On their feet, the most of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-273-Teague went on down to the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:So he had it, and there was nothing in it that a man could not have read off a yard and a keeper. They had cleared a ford with five, in a way that nobody on those roads had cleared one before, and they had walked a mountain and brought back a true answer; and they had lost a cart doing it, and come down with three of them marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-277-He had four things to say to that boy. He priced each of them, on the bank, as he would have priced a stage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:279:The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-281-The second cost Teague something, and he had known that he would pay it since the fifty-seventh day. The ford had been good work. To say so aloud was to say that the slip had gone where it ought, and that he had no claim left on it, even in his own head. It was true, and a true thing was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-282-
