editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:15:He said it low, for the file. His strides were yards, and he had paced this lane at noon behind a cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:19:He had been waiting for it, and it was still a bad thing to see. On the south side, a few yards out from the lane, the seed-heads bent and stood and bent again in a slow line that went along beside the file. When he turned his head they were doing the same on the north. No shape showed in it anywhere; there was only the grass leaning, a little at a time, at exactly the pace of a girl putting down the whole of her foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:65:She turned her head and looked at him. It was nearly dark, and he could see only the pale shape of her face. He thought that she had heard something that was not about the order of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:87:It was the dark-backed one. It came out low and from the flank, at the smallest thing in the file, with none of the idleness that it had shown on a lip. He had time to think that Karis walked alone, and that he had been a fool not to see what that would look like from the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:43:Oryn looked at him for as long as it took her to draw one breath. She had seen a boy burst sideways out of a file with a crack like a whip, in a place where she herself could not so much as count; she had seen fire in his fist. He waited for what every person with a seal had asked him since a district hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:269:He could not make it. He had the fact, which was that nine things had answered him in a place where a healer could not count. He had nothing to put after the fact. And he knew what became of a thing that was written down before anybody understood it, since it had been done to him. A clerk had found no tier for a boy, and had put a word in capitals where the tier should go, and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was. A file stopped the asking. He would not do that to this, and he would not hide it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:273:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:177:[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:There was the other thing, and he came to it when he had finished with himself, as he came to the difficult page of any file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:249:"The Compact knows what quiet ground does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:253:"And it's filed somewhere we can't reach."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:263:"I carried the maps. I never saw the file. I'm not going to draw you one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:143:Cael did not see them go. He had the whole of his attention in a pace and a half of turf, feeling for the next weight to come onto the rim above Brom, and he had let his eyes follow it down; and when he lifted them the file had stopped.

