editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md:154:## 49. Book 7 — protected Quiet-line / Kindling wording (OWNER ITEM; needed before Book 7 Movement 4)
editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md-155-Seven protected lines say variants of "mine didn't", "my Path", "mine has been asleep since I was fourteen", "dark since fourteen", "every Path … his had not". Edition canon (Book 6): Cael's own Arbiter woke at Kindling, the plate lit eleven seconds late, the failed-safe word replaced the old name, and the Arbiter went dark; he has no Path. At a Quiet line the others' live Arbiters stop, his is dark before and after, and his fragments still answer. **Default until ruled: the protected lines stay verbatim**, the surrounding plan and prose state the canon, and the lines read as Cael's own shorthand. Options: (1) release only the conflicting clauses for a minimal canon repair; (2) release the affected log entries for clean re-composition; (3) keep as is (the default).
editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md:157:**#49 update (coordinator, 2026-10-11, before B7 Movement 4).** Read closely, most of the seven lines already agree with canon as written: the first says the others' ARBITERS stopped and "Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen."; the fourth says "whatever I am isn't a Path"; Karis's struck line says "one dark since fourteen; no change either side of the line". Only two use "Path … mine" loosely ("Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't"; "every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't"). Default confirmed: all seven stay verbatim as Cael's log shorthand; Movement 4's packet carries a binding note that narration and dialogue never say "his Path" and state the canon exactly (the five live Arbiters stop; his has been dark since Kindling; the fragments still answer). If the owner prefers, only those two clauses need releasing.
